routes.brief:
The atlas has Shimura varieties with Hecke correspondences (ShimuraVarieties V0–V7) and plans André–Oort-type applications in LogicAndDefinabilityInNumberTheory LD.6. It has nothing on generalised Hecke orbits, uniform integral Tate hypotheses or Galois lower bounds for Hecke orbits.

Import:
- Shimura data and varieties from ShimuraData and ShimuraVarieties;
- the Pila–Zannier machinery (o-minimality, counting, functional transcendence) from LD.6;
- hyperspecial models and the Cartan decomposition from ReductiveGroupsPartII RG2.3–RG2.4;
- uniform integral Faltings (Theorem 4.7) and Masser–Wüstholz from FaltingsFinitenessAndIsogenyTheorems R28.4;
- Serre's ℓ-independence and Noot's specialization from ArithmeticGaloisRepresentations R01.6;
- flatness and lifting of points from SchemeAndStackFoundations SF.0;
- Goursat's lemma from Mathlib.

First construct, following Richard–Yafaev, Height functions on Hecke orbits (arXiv:2109.13718): generalised and geometric Hecke orbits, the height H_f on W(𝔸_f) with its index comparisons, and the reduction of Conjecture 1.1 to Galois lower bounds (its Theorem 1.2).

Then state and prove, as in Richard–Yafaev (Publ. Math. IHÉS 141 (2025)):
- the uniform integral Tate property (Definitions 2.1–2.4) with Nori theory, Serre's complete reducibility and McNinch;
- its functoriality (Propositions 4.1–4.6, Corollaries 4.10–4.11);
- Theorem 5.1 via Propositions 5.2, 5.5, 5.7, Theorem 5.4 and the structure Lemmas 5.9–5.19, including a correct Lemma 5.12 (sourceIssues E6);
- Theorem 6.1 with §8's convexity estimates;
- the p-adic Kempf–Ness Theorem 7.1 over ℤ_p with Seshadri's GIT;
- Appendices A–B;
- Theorems 1.4 and 1.3.

Export Theorem 1.3 to LD.6 as a proven Zilber–Pink case. Acceptance: for S-Hecke orbits §5.2 alone suffices (Remark 5.2.1); the bounds hold for every point of A_g.
